    "tree_id": "a91774b36adf18241db9d0877d29bd5c6bed493e",
    "message": "fix(maint71): a cancelled refresh that never started does not hold the next one (#4552)\n\n* fix(maint71): a cancelled refresh that never started does not hold the next one\n\n#4492 exempted one pre-sync infrastructure failure from the six-hour canary refresh hold, but a\ncancelled run still held it even when no Sync job ever ran. The stable-writer concurrency queue\nreplaces pending Maint 68 runs, so a replaced run latched the canaries: on 2026-11-07 01:39Z the\nguard printed \"refresh: skipped (cancelled)\" with confirmed_not_started=2, and only force=true\nmoved them. A cancelled run with job evidence showing no Sync job, or a not-started receipt, is now\nnot an attempt and is reported as cancelled_before_sync. Missing job evidence still holds.\n\nTest: a cancelled refresh that never started a Sync job does not debounce the next attempt.\nDeliberate break (exclusion removed): that test fails, 225/226; restored 226/226.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(maint71): count receipt-only cancelled runs and test the cancelled-job read path\n\nReview on #4552: a cancelled run whose job list is unreadable but whose receipt says it never started\nis now counted as cancelled_before_sync, and a readCanaryRefreshState test proves cancelled runs fetch\ntheir attempt's jobs (removing that fetch fails it: 226/227).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
    "timestamp": "2026-11-08T16:08:39Z",
